  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Marriage, Birth, and Life-Event Timelines",
    "description": "Anchor each clue to the wedding, birth, past date, present, or future before comparing ages.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use short, connected phone-sized teaching screens with a concrete age situation before the equation, label every past/present/future date clearly, show the reason for each age shift, and check the result in the original clue. After each worked example, give a fresh near-transfer question. Wrong answers receive a misconception-specific correction and a parallel retry.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 8, Problems on Ages. Approved scope: Teach life-event age problems by treating marriage, births, and family milestones as dated anchors. Cover finding age at marriage from 'married n years ago', using a parent's age at a child's birth as a permanent age gap, comparing parents at the same child's birth date, finding a child's age from years between marriage and birth, solving a marriage-age ratio by shifting both spouses back the same number of years, and handling clues that compare one person's past age with other people's present ages. Include multi-generation chains and a capstone with several event dates. Emphasize one stage at a time and check elapsed years between events."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Marriage and birth questions are still timeline questions. The event gives you a date anchor. Once that anchor is clear, the age arithmetic becomes ordinary addition and subtraction.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Farah married 8 years ago. If she was x at marriage, she is x+8 today.\n\nIf today’s age is 36, her age at marriage was 36−8=28.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A life event does not change how age works. It simply tells you which date a clue belongs to.\n\nLabel the event date first, then move to today or another date.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
